# S12.1 final-review delta: Git hardlinks, rollback durability and ORIG_HEAD

Date: 2026-09-30
Scope: source-only correction before merge/freeze/deploy
Deployment attempts consumed: 0

## Findings

1. The root Git metadata walk rejected symlinks and special files but did not
   reject regular files with multiple hard links. A root Git write could
   therefore mutate an external alias.
2. Repository data was `syncfs`-flushed while the recovery barrier was active,
   but ownership/mode restoration occurred while leaving the barrier. The
   rollback-complete marker could therefore follow metadata changes that had
   not received a final filesystem flush.
3. Backup/rollback captured HEAD, release branches and the managed freeze tag,
   but not the Git pseudo-ref `ORIG_HEAD`. A failed attempt could consequently
   leave a rollback-side `ORIG_HEAD` mutation behind.
4. The Git metadata hardlink gate did not cover regular files in the canonical
   worktrees. An existing external hardlink could remain writable after the
   repository parent and root permissions were locked.

## Resolution

- Require every regular entry in the complete quarantined Git metadata tree to
  have link count one before any root Git operation.
- Flush both canonical repository filesystems again after barrier removal and
  ownership/mode restoration, before clearing the barrier journal or writing
  rollback completion evidence.
- Record `ORIG_HEAD`, include a present value in the authenticated bundle,
  validate its object, and restore and verify either the exact value or exact
  absence. The restore-index schema advances to V5 so an older record cannot
  be interpreted as an intentional absent value.
- After locking the shared repository parent and rejecting retained handles,
  require link count one for every regular file throughout both worktrees
  before locking their roots or running root Git.

The correction does not alter product boundaries, credential handling, the
single-attempt contract or deployment scope.
